{"level":30,"time":1776368901200,"pid":59252,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"13.md","index":8,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VWpnEYDpU7ApMtxtSxXqAy","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Electric Review\n     2→\n     3→*Lists the preliminary design requirements and links for Austin Energy review comments.*\n     4→\n     5→Electric Review – Rosemary Avila (512) 972-8488 - rosemary.avila@austinenergy.com\n     6→\n     7→See Bluebeam link below to download AE site comments:\n     8→Link: https://studio.bluebeam.com/share/epcafnizivuefw\n     9→Applicant, for your comment responses use the color BLUE.\n    10→\n    11→Site Plan Preliminary Design Requirements\n    12→Austin Energy Design Criteria Effective December 12th, 2023\n    13→* UCM § 1.3.8 - Customer Electrical Facilities that Cross Property Lines\n    14→* UCM § 1.3.12 - AE Line Extension Policy\n    15→* UCM § 1.3.16- Truck Access to AE Construction and Existing Facility Sites\n    16→* UCM § 1.9.3 - Installation of Metering Equipment\n    17→* UCM § 1.10.3 - Permanent Clearances from AE Overhead Lines and Facilities\n    18→* UCM § 1.10.4 - Clearances from AE Padmount Equipment and Distribution Vaults\n    19→* UCM § 1.10.10.3 & 4 - Customer Activity in Utility Easements - Landscaping & Trees\n    20→* Appendix F of the Environmental Criteria Manual\n    21→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"99486be3-3f6f-4f49-a719-066c2262255d","uuid":"e1594d82-a9d0-42be-87f5-f4f75cc13994","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/wnavey/code/controlroom/conductor/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/supplementary-docs/master-comment-report/02-electric-review.md","content":"# Electric Review\n\n*Lists the preliminary design requirements and links for Austin Energy review comments.*\n\nElectric Review – Rosemary Avila (512) 972-8488 - rosemary.avila@austinenergy.com\n\nSee Bluebeam link below to download AE site comments:\nLink: https://studio.bluebeam.com/share/epcafnizivuefw\nApplicant, for your comment responses use the color BLUE.\n\nSite Plan Preliminary Design Requirements\nAustin Energy Design Criteria Effective December 12th, 2023\n* UCM § 1.3.8 - Customer Electrical Facilities that Cross Property Lines\n* UCM § 1.3.12 - AE Line Extension Policy\n* UCM § 1.3.16- Truck Access to AE Construction and Existing Facility Sites\n* UCM § 1.9.3 - Installation of Metering Equipment\n* UCM § 1.10.3 - Permanent Clearances from AE Overhead Lines and Facilities\n* UCM § 1.10.4 - Clearances from AE Padmount Equipment and Distribution Vaults\n* UCM § 1.10.10.3 & 4 - Customer Activity in Utility Easements - Landscaping & Trees\n* Appendix F of the Environmental Criteria Manual\n","numLines":21,"startLine":1,"totalLines":21}}}
{"level":30,"time":1776368901200,"pid":59252,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"13.md","index":8,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_0151ngTB2upiuZDM4zL26XAE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good. The Electric Review references UCM § 1.10.4 (Clearances from AE Padmount Equipment and Distribution Vaults), which is exactly what my grouping covers. The review comments are in a Bluebeam link that's external, but I can at least see that Austin Energy has flagged several UCM sections as preliminary design requirements.\n\nNow I need to do a detailed analysis. Let me summarize what I've found so far:\n\n**RESEARCH SUMMARY:**\n\n**Transformer Pads Identified:**\n- 4 transformer pads shown on Sheet 21 (Electrical Plan)\n  1. Northwest: west of Building 2\n  2. West-central: between Buildings 2 and 8\n  3. East-central: east of Building 4 (between Buildings 5 and 6)\n  4. South-central: south of Building 7\n\n**Clearance Dimensions Found:**\n- Sheet 21 shows dashed rectangles around pads with at least one \"5'\" dimension shown (between northwest pad and a line to the east - possibly building)\n- Other clearances not explicitly dimensioned on the plans\n\n**Trees and Vegetation:**\n- Landscape plan shows trees throughout the site but doesn't explicitly show transformer pad locations\n- No root barrier details visible on landscape sheets\n- Tree list needs detailed review for species and proximity\n\n**Utilities:**\n- Sheet 19 (Utility Tap Plan) shows water, wastewater, storm drain, and overhead electric utilities\n- Shows legend indicating \"T\" symbol for Transformer Pad\n- But transformer pad exact locations/distances to utilities not clearly documented\n\n**Missing Information:**\n- No Austin Energy Design approval documents (transformer pad detail sheets, ESPA, written approvals for clearance reductions)\n- No transformer pad dimensions specified\n- No transformer type specified (oil-filled vs. dry-type, single-phase vs. three-phase)\n- No bollard details shown\n- No drainage slope analysis relative to transformer pads\n\n\n- Fire escape and stairwell locations not documented, niche configurations absent, and vertical clearance cross-sections for overhead obstructions missing\n\n**Critical Gaps:**\n\nThe plans show transformer pads but lack the required Austin Energy Design detail sheets. Clearance dimensions are sparse—only the 5' measurement is annotated, while others remain unverified. Pad specifications (dimensions, transformer type) are completely absent, and I need to check whether any trees fall within the 20-foot clearance zone.\n\nThe utility plan shows routing but doesn't indicate clearance distances from transformers to water, wastewater, storm drain (5 feet required), or other utilities (12 inches required). I also need to verify the grading plan confirms surface drainage slopes away from oil-filled pads, and determine whether transformers are oil-filled or dry-type, since this affects fire escape clearances, drainage, and window/door setbacks.\n\nI should now measure specific clearances between transformers and nearby features to verify compliance.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_tokens":653,"cache_read_input_tokens":66775,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":653},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"99486be3-3f6f-4f49-a719-066c2262255d","uuid":"911773cf-9fa7-4333-aabb-830f85bdd863"}
