{"level":30,"time":1776370143457,"pid":59252,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":6,"runIndex":"run-2","documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNum":31,"prompt":"Examine the landscape plan and identify: 1) Overhead electric (OHE) line routing shown on the plan; 2) Location of utility poles and guy wires; 3) Proposed tree and landscape planting locations relative to the overhead electric lines; 4) Tree species identified (check if any are non-utility-compatible species placed near overhead lines); 5) Any clearance dimensions from trees, landscaping, or structures to the overhead electric lines; 6) Whether tree species comply with utility-compatible requirements for placement within clearance zones. Report specifically which tree species are placed near overhead utilities and whether clearance dimensions are annotated.","res":{"steps":[{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on a visual analysis of the provided landscape plan (Sheet 28 of 35 for \"VALLEY VIEW TOWNHOMES\"), here are the findings regarding your specific queries:\n\n**1) Overhead electric (OHE) line routing shown on the plan:**\n*   An overhead electric line is visible along the southern (bottom) boundary of the site plan. It is routed horizontally parallel to the property line and is explicitly denoted by a dashed line interspersed with the text label **\"- OHE -\"**.\n\n**2) Location of utility poles and guy wires:**\n*   There are no explicit labels, callouts, or legend entries for \"utility poles\" or \"guy wires\" on this specific sheet. While there are some small circular symbols along the property boundaries, without a specific legend key confirming them as utility poles (as opposed to boundary markers or other features), their exact locations cannot be definitively confirmed from this page alone.\n\n**3) Proposed tree and landscape planting locations relative to the overhead electric lines:**\n*   There are several proposed trees placed in very close proximity to, or directly underneath, the **\"- OHE -\"** routing along the southern boundary of the site. Specifically, there are at least four prominent tree canopy circles drawn directly over or immediately adjacent to the OHE line.\n\n**4) Tree species identified (check if any are non-utility-compatible species placed near overhead lines):**\n*   The trees placed near the overhead electric lines are tagged with the letter **\"M\"**.\n*   According to the legend located below the \"KEY NOTES,\" the symbol **\"M\"** corresponds to **\"MITIGATION TREE\"**.\n*   **Specific botanical or common species names (e.g., Oak, Elm, Crepe Myrtle) are NOT provided** on this sheet. Because the actual plant species are not identified in a plant schedule on this page, it is impossible to determine from this document alone whether these proposed \"Mitigation Trees\" are non-utility-compatible species.\n\n**5) Any clearance dimensions from trees, landscaping, or structures to the overhead electric lines:**\n*   **No clearance dimensions are annotated.** There are no visible dimension lines or callouts indicating the required or proposed horizontal or vertical clearance distances between the proposed trees and the overhead electric lines.\n\n**6) Whether tree species comply with utility-compatible requirements for placement within clearance zones:**\n*   Compliance cannot be verified from this sheet. As noted above, the specific tree species are not identified (only categorized generically as \"MITIGATION TREE\"), and clearance dimensions are absent. Therefore, a determination of utility compatibility cannot be made based solely on this page of the submission.","providerMetadata":{"google":{"thoughtSignature":""}}}],"finishReason":"stop","usage":{"inputTokens":1438,"outputTokens":546,"totalTokens":4235,"reasoningTokens":2251},"warnings":[],"request":{"body":{"prompt":[{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"file","mediaType":"image/jpeg","data":"[BINARY_DATA_REMOVED - length: 1755680]"},{"type":"text","text":"# Your task\n\nYou are analyzing a subset of a real estate development site plan submission document.\n\nVisually analyze the binary file provided with extreme accuracy. Pay close attention to tables, reproducing them EXACTLY as they appear, character-by-character.\n\nCRITICAL: Do not paraphrase, interpret, or \"fill in\" text. If you cannot read something clearly, state \"text unclear\" rather than guessing. Transcribe field labels and values exactly as written.\n\nIf the requirements being validated require certain content to be present, look closely for it and provide all relevant information that pertains to the broader request. Remember that you are only looking at a single supplementary document, or a single page from the primary site plan. Just because you do not find evidence for a requirement on this page does not mean it will not be present on another page.\n\nReport back precisely on what you see, using exact quotes for any text you transcribe from forms, tables, or labels.\n\nThe final review agent will not be able to look at this document directly, so you must be their eyes. Ensure you describe everything relevant in full detail with perfect accuracy.\n"},{"type":"text","text":"Examine the landscape plan and identify: 1) Overhead electric (OHE) line routing shown on the plan; 2) Location of utility poles and guy wires; 3) Proposed tree and landscape planting locations relative to the overhead electric lines; 4) Tree species identified (check if any are non-utility-compatible species placed near overhead lines); 5) Any clearance dimensions from trees, landscaping, or structures to the overhead electric lines; 6) Whether tree species comply with utility-compatible requirements for placement within clearance zones. Report specifically which tree species are placed near overhead utilities and whether clearance dimensions are annotated."}]}],"headers":{"user-agent":"ai/5.0.152"}}},"response":{"id":"aitxt-92sV5IV7M3y1J